{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"subjective-forgeable-banteng","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context\n\nFound while investigating the CI-flake cluster (bugs 168e / 50b1 / ac76 / cb9c). This failure\nis not covered by any of those tickets and recurs across runs.\n\n`tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py::test_matching_guard_is_accepted` hard-CRASHES its xdist\nworker — the worker process dies rather than the test failing an assertion:\n\n- run 31266912051, job 93126525096 (ubuntu py3.11):\n  `worker 'gw3' crashed while running 'tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py::test_matching_guard_is_accepted'`,\n  preceded by `[gw3] node down: Not properly terminated`.\n  Totals `1 failed, 11435 passed, 66 skipped in 879.17s`.\n- run 31290743251, job 93187384196 (ubuntu py3.11):\n  same test, `worker 'gw1' crashed`. Totals `1 failed, 11560 passed, 66 skipped in 901.74s`.\n\nTwo different workers, two different runs, the same test — so it is the test, not the worker slot.\n\n## What is known\n\n- **No traceback in either log.** The worker died without a Python-level exception.\n- The logs contain **no** `Segmentation fault`, `Fatal Python error`, or `INTERNALERROR` text,\n  so the usual native-crash markers are absent.\n- Both occurrences are on **ubuntu py3.11** under `-n 4 --dist worksteal`.\n- Both runs grep clean for `No space` / `ENOSPC` / `Cannot allocate` / runner warnings, so this\n  is not visible disk or memory pressure at the runner level.\n- The jobs that crashed are among the slower ones in their runs (879s and 902s versus ~610-640s\n  for the sibling legs), consistent with a resource- or timing-sensitive path.\n\nA silent worker death with no Python traceback and no native-crash marker usually means the\nprocess was killed (OOM-killer or a signal) or exited via a hard `os._exit` / native abort in\nsomething the test loads. `test_opcert_service_app.py` exercises a service app, so a spawned\nserver/subprocess or a native dependency are the first places to look.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The crash is reproduced, or the worker's cause of death is captured (exit signal / dmesg\n      OOM evidence / faulthandler output).\n- [ ] The mechanism is classified: OOM kill, native abort, deliberate process exit, or xdist\n      protocol failure.\n- [ ] `test_matching_guard_is_accepted` no longer takes down its xdist worker, without weakening\n      what it asserts.\n- [ ] The test is stable across repeated `-n 4 --dist worksteal` runs on py3.11.","id":"c89f-08b1-2e8c-4133","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["ci","flake"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Flaky CI: test_matching_guard_is_accepted kills its xdist worker with no traceback"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786502365868144001,"uuid":"33bf9c94-0ade-4f82-9056-0f948f41fc71"}